<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
            http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
      291 — account.mastodon_localpart: the RESIDUAL half of the
      account-takeover-by-collision fix (changeset 290 closed the Matrix half).

      BUG: changeset 290 made account.localpart (the ServiceTokenBroker.matrixUser
      fold — charset [a-z0-9._=/-], no truncation) UNIQUE, so no two accounts can
      share a Matrix localpart. But the Mastodon/Akkoma mint path RE-FOLDS that
      canonical value through the strictly-COARSER ServiceTokenBroker.mastodonLocalpart
      (folds '.','-','=','/' -> '_' and 30-char-caps, because Mastodon rejects the
      '.-=/' Matrix permits and caps usernames at 30). So two DISTINCT, 290-unique
      accounts — e.g. localpart 'john.doe' and 'john-doe', or any two sharing a 30-char
      prefix — collapse to the SAME Mastodon localpart 'john_doe'. Because both BFFs'
      ServiceTokenBroker recover-leg (Mastodon OAuth password grant) logs in with ONE
      SHARED service password, whichever account is PROVISIONED SECOND silently
      password-logs into the FIRST account's already-registered Mastodon identity — the
      exact takeover 290 closed for Matrix, still open on the coarser Mastodon fold.

      FIX (same shape as 290: provision-time uniqueness, read-persisted-never-rederive):
      persist the RESOLVED, collision-checked Mastodon localpart on the account row too,
      at the SAME one chokepoint every account is created through
      (AccountLocalpartService.resolveAndPersist, called by registerGoogle /
      registerDiscord / InternalAccountController#ensureAccount — the only 3 writers of
      new account rows), and have every Mastodon-mint call site READ this persisted
      value (AuthController#provision here; user-bff-api's MastodonProxyController + the
      media leg, over the internal account-read API) rather than re-folding whatever
      localpart is at hand at mint time. A candidate that collides with a DIFFERENT
      account's persisted mastodon_localpart is a 409 at registration — identical UX to
      290's Matrix collision, one fold coarser.

      FOLD-STABILITY INVARIANT (the reason the backfill suffix below is NOT 290's
      '-'||md5 form): the persisted mastodon_localpart is fed to the homeserver mint,
      which defensively re-applies mastodonLocalpart() to it. For uniqueness on the
      STORED value to equal uniqueness at the HOMESERVER, the stored value MUST be a
      FIXED POINT of mastodonLocalpart — i.e. already only [a-z0-9_], length <= 30
      (mastodonLocalpart(x) == x). 290's disambiguator 'candidate || '-' || md5' is NOT
      mastodon-safe (the '-' re-folds to '_'), which would let two distinct STORED values
      re-fold to the same homeserver localpart and resurrect the collision. So the
      backfill here folds the base, truncates to leave room, and suffixes with
      '_' || short-id-hash (all [a-z0-9_], total <= 30) — a fold fixed point.

      DELIBERATELY NULLABLE (mirrors 290): AccountStore.ensureAccount (auth-core, an
      external versioned dependency) INSERTs via an entity whose column list excludes
      this column, so NOT NULL would fail every account creation; a DEFAULT would break
      the UNIQUE constraint the moment two accounts were created. Postgres UNIQUE treats
      NULLs as pairwise-distinct, so nullable + UNIQUE is correct: resolveAndPersist
      fills it within the SAME request as the INSERT, and #provision is unreachable
      until the separate registration-complete step, so a real caller never observes a
      null on its own account.

      BACKFILL: derives each row's mastodon_localpart from its ALREADY-backfilled
      (changeset 290) account.localpart via the SQL mirror of mastodonLocalpart
      (regexp_replace '[^a-z0-9_]' -> '_' , substr 1..30, all-underscore -> 'user'),
      then deterministically disambiguates any collision AMONG EXISTING rows by
      updated_at then id (the only ordering columns the account table carries — same
      imperfect-but-only proxy for first-created that 290 documents), first-created
      keeping the bare candidate, later ones suffixed with the fold-stable
      '_' || substr(md5(id),1,8). Only rows whose localpart is already set are
      backfilled (a null localpart yields a null mastodon_localpart — self-heals on the
      next read that needs it, exactly like 290). Exercised against live Postgres
      semantics by the same DataConstraintsTest / AccountStoreTest slices as 290; no
      cross-fold collisions exist in any committed fixture (compose personas are
      single-provider e2e-* handles with no '.'/'-'), but the UNIQUE constraint is the
      loud backstop if the backfill ever did produce one.

      Band 291: next free above 290. Appended LAST — account (004) + localpart (290)
      both exist by here, so addColumn + backfill + unique apply cleanly.
    -->

    <changeSet id="291-account-mastodon-localpart-add-column" author="hugmesh">
        <comment>account.mastodon_localpart — nullable (see rationale above), the
                 resolved collision-checked Mastodon/Akkoma localpart for this account
                 (the coarser [a-z0-9_], 30-cap fold on top of account.localpart).</comment>
        <addColumn tableName="account">
            <column name="mastodon_localpart" type="varchar(64)"/>
        </addColumn>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="291-account-mastodon-localpart-backfill" author="hugmesh">
        <comment>Backfill mastodon_localpart from the already-resolved account.localpart
                 with the SQL mirror of ServiceTokenBroker.mastodonLocalpart; collisions
                 among existing rows disambiguated by updated_at/id order, later ones
                 suffixed with a FOLD-STABLE '_'||md5(id)[0:8] (never 290's '-' form —
                 see the fold-stability invariant above) so the stored value is a fixed
                 point of mastodonLocalpart. Only rows with a non-null localpart.</comment>
        <sql>
            WITH candidates AS (
                SELECT id, updated_at,
                       CASE
                           WHEN regexp_replace(substr(regexp_replace(lower(localpart), '[^a-z0-9_]', '_', 'g'), 1, 30), '_', '', 'g') = ''
                           THEN 'user'
                           ELSE substr(regexp_replace(lower(localpart), '[^a-z0-9_]', '_', 'g'), 1, 30)
                       END AS candidate
                FROM account
                WHERE localpart IS NOT NULL
            ), ranked AS (
                SELECT id, candidate,
                       ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY candidate ORDER BY updated_at ASC, id ASC) AS rn
                FROM candidates
            )
            UPDATE account a
            SET mastodon_localpart = CASE
                    WHEN r.rn = 1 THEN r.candidate
                    ELSE substr(r.candidate, 1, 21) || '_' || substr(lower(md5(r.id)), 1, 8)
                END
            FROM ranked r
            WHERE a.id = r.id;
        </sql>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="291-account-mastodon-localpart-unique" author="hugmesh">
        <comment>UNIQUE(mastodon_localpart) — the DB-level backstop (NULLs exempt); the
                 per-call-site collision check in AccountLocalpartService is the
                 application-level guard, this makes a colliding mint impossible to
                 persist either way.</comment>
        <addUniqueConstraint tableName="account" columnNames="mastodon_localpart"
                             constraintName="uq_account_mastodon_localpart"/>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
